Since provider.tf already exists, you should not repeat the terraform block or provider "aws" block in main.tf.

Your existing provider.tf already has:

AWS provider version 5.91.0
Region us-east-1
IAM endpoint http://aws:4566
The other LocalStack endpoints

So main.tf only needs the IAM policy resource.

/home/bob/terraform/main.tf

Create/edit it:

cd /home/bob/terraform
vi main.tf

Put only this inside:

resource "aws_iam_policy" "iampolicy_mark" {
  name        = "iampolicy_mark"
  description = "Read-only access to EC2 console"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"

    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"

        Action = [
          "ec2:DescribeInstances",
          "ec2:DescribeImages",
          "ec2:DescribeSnapshots"
        ]

        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
Then run
terraform fmt

Then:

terraform validate

Then:

terraform apply

Type:

yes
Why we don't add the provider again

Terraform automatically combines all .tf files in the same directory.

So your directory effectively becomes:

/home/bob/terraform/
├── provider.tf    ← AWS provider configuration
└── main.tf        ← IAM policy

Terraform reads both files together.

If you put another terraform {} or provider "aws" {} block in main.tf, you'd risk the duplicate provider configuration problem you encountered previously.

Also, your existing provider.tf already contains:

iam = "http://aws:4566"

so the IAM policy will use the correct LocalStack endpoint.
